<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s07e03" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 3: Multiplying with Variables, Part 1</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 3 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Multiply one term by another term.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use distribution to multiply a term by a polynomial.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 3 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Distribution
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Remember from Season 1 that an exponent means repeated multiplication:
    <md>
      x^4 = x\cdot x \cdot x \cdot x
    </md>
    Remembering the meaning of exponents allows us to multiply expressions that contain variables.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Simplify:
      <md>
        x^3 \cdot x^4
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: The term <m>x^{3}</m> consists of 3 multiplied x’s, and the term <m>x^{4}</m> consists of 4 multiplied x’s:
      <md>
        (x\cdot x \cdot x) \cdot (x\cdot x \cdot x \cdot x)
      </md>
      In total, we see we have 7 multiplied x’s, so the final answer is:
      <md>
        x^7
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
  <p>
    Simplify:
    <md>
      2x(5x^3)
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Solution: Unpack the exponents:
    <md>
      2\cdot x \cdot 5 \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x
    </md>
    All together, we have a coefficient of <m>2\cdot 5=10</m>, and we have 4 multiplied x’s, so the final answer is:
    <md>
      10x^4
    </md>
  </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Simplify:
      <md>
        -3x^4(-7x^2)
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Unpack the exponents:
      <md>
        (-3)\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x \cdot (-7)\cdot x \cdot x
      </md>
      We have a coefficient of <m>(-3)\cdot (-7)=21</m>, and we have 6 multiplied x’s, so the final answer is:
      <md>
        21x^6
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Be Careful!</title>
    <p>
      Notice the difference between <m>-3x^4(-7x^2)</m> and <m>-3x^4 -7x^2</m>?
    </p>
    <p>
      In the first expression, the - sign next to 7 means negative or opposite. That’s because there is a ( symbol to the left of that - sign (see Season 02).
    </p>
    <p>
      In the second expression, the - sign next to 7 means subtract. There’s because there is value to the left of the -.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first expression can be simplified:
      <md>
        -3x^4(-7x^2) = 21x^6
      </md>
      But notice that the second expression cannot, because the terms are not like:
      <md>
        -3x^4 -7x^2
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <definition xml:id="def-distribution">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For problems that contain a mixture of addition, subtraction, and multiplication, <term>distribution</term> 
        tells us how multiplication interacts with addition and subtraction. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
<example>
  <p>
    Simplify:
    <md>
      2(x^3+5)
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Solution: Here, we are asked to take the quantity <m>x^{3}+5</m> and multiply that quantity by 2. 
    Instead, we will separately multiply <m>x^{3}</m> by 2 and 5 by 2. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In other words, we distribute a copy of 2 to each term inside the parentheses. 
    <md>
      2\cdot x^3 + 2\cdot 5
    </md>
    <md>
      2x^3 + 10
    </md>
  </p>
</example>
<assemblage>
  <title>Makes Sense, Right?</title>
  <p>
    Imagine you get a snack pack containing 4 carrots and <m>x</m> cookies:
    <md>
      4+x
    </md>
    If you get two snack packs:
    <md>
      2(4+x)
    </md>
    then you’ll end up with double the number of carrots and double the number of cookies:
    <md>
      2\cdot 4 + 2\cdot x
    </md>
    This is why we can distribute to each term inside the parentheses.
  </p>
</assemblage>
<example>
  <p>
    Simplify:
    <md>
      x^3(x^4-x^2+7)
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    Solution: We distribute a copy of <m>x^{3}</m> to each term inside the parentheses:
    <md>
      x^3\cdot x^4 - x^3 \cdot x^2 + x^3 \cdot 7
    </md>
    Now, let’s simplify each term:
    <md>
      x^3\cdot x^4 = (x\cdot x \cdot x) \cdot (x\cdot x \cdot x \cdot x) = x^7
    </md>
    <md>
      x^3\cdot x^2 = (x\cdot x \cdot x) \cdot (x\cdot x) = x^5
    </md>
    <md>
      x^3 \cdot 7 = 7x^3
    </md>
    So our final answer is:
    <md>
      x^7-x^5+7x^3
    </md>
  </p>
</example>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-s07e03">
  <title>Season 7, Episode 3 Practice Problems</title>
  <p>
    Simplify the following:
  </p>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>3x^2\cdot 5x^4</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>7c^5\cdot (-0.4c^2)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-4a^3\cdot 2a</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-9n\cdot \left(-\frac{1}{3}n^6\right)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>6m(-3m^2))</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>1.2k^3\cdot 0.5k^2</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>0.5y^2\cdot 8y</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>\frac{4}{7}r\cdot 14r^4</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>\frac{2}{3}b^4\cdot 9b</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-11d^2\cdot d^3</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-\frac{5}{6}p^2\cdot \frac{12}{5}p^3</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>2.5q^4(-8q)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <p>
    Simplify the following:
  </p>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>3x(2x+5-x^2)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>7c^3(0.4c^2+5-2c+c^4)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-4a(3a-7+2a^2)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-9n^2\left(n-\frac{1}{3}+4n^3\right)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>6m^2(m+4-2m^3+1)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>1.2k(5k^2-0.5k+3)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>0.5y(8y-6+2y^2)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>\frac{4}{7}r^2(14r+7-r^3+2)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>\frac{2}{3}b^2(9b+3-6b^2)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-11d(-d^2+3-4d+d^3)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>-\frac{5}{6}p(12p-6+3p^2-9)</m></p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.83in">
    <p><m>2.5q^3(-8q+4+2q^2-1)</m></p>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
</section> 